    "summary": "Monster Playing‑in‑the‑Band jam, unstoppable Big River, flawless China > Rider",
    "blurb": "The set rides a smooth groove with a monster 25‑minute Playing In The Band stretch and a blistering Big River solo. China Cat Sunflower into I Know You Rider shines with crystal‑clear guitars, while the second half erupts into an epic Truckin’ → Other One → Wharf Rat corridor. Overall performance is tight, energetic, and captured in excellent audio.",
    "review": "July 1, 1973 at the Universal Amphitheatre delivered a quintessential ’73 Dead experience. The first set builds nicely with folk‑rock staples like Mississippi Half‑Step and Sugaree before launching into a searing Big River where Garcia’s solo is described as “unstoppable” and “liquid fire.” The China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue glides with crisp, soaring guitars that many reviewers call “prime Garcia” and “sweet sweet geetar.”\n\nThe second set is the show’s high‑water mark: a 25‑minute Playing In The Band jam that propels Phil Lesh’s bass into overdrive, followed by an awe‑inspiring Truckin’ → The Other One → Wharf Rat marathon. Drummers Bill Kreutzmann (and in some mixes Mickey Hart) provide a strong, driving pulse that underpins the improvisations. Across soundboard, matrix, and audience sources the performance remains consistently electrifying, with multiple recordings praised for excellent mix clarity and minimal technical flaws.",
